        super.onDestroy();
        Intent intent = new Intent(this, NextActivity.class); // Adjust for your specific activity
        startActivity(intent);
    }
    ```

This alteration will ensure that the lifecycle events of the first activity are properly completed, thus assisting in properly releasing any resources and preventing memory leaks. Since you noted that memory usage does not grow when the `onDestroy()` of the previous activity runs before the `onResume()` of the new one, this approach aligns well with ensuring that lifecycle events occur in the correct order, which is crucial to managing memory efficiently in Android ([SO-63033583]).

By taking such measures, you will be able to mitigate the memory leak and keep your application's memory footprint in check while switching between activities.

